(since dividing by 125 and taking mod 1000 requires knowing the value mod 125×1000 = 125000).\n\nWait, let me think. If M = 12^2015 + 13^2015, and 125 | M, then M/125 mod 1000 requires M mod 125000.\n\nBecause M/125 mod 1000 = (M mod 125000)/125, as long as 125 | M (which it does since n=3 means 125 | M).\n\nSo I need 12^2015 + 13^2015 mod 125000.\n\n125000 = 125 × 1000 = 5^3 × 2^3 × 5^3 = 2^3 × 5^6 = 8 × 15625.\n\nSo 125000 = 8 × 15625.\n\nBy CRT, I need M mod 8 and M mod 15625.\n\nM mod 8: 12 ≡ 4 mod 8, 13 ≡ 5 mod 8.\n12^2015 mod 8: 12 = 4×3, 12^2 = 144 ≡ 0 mod 8. So 12^2015 ≡ 0 mod 8 (since 2015 ≥ 2).\n13^2015 mod 8: 13 ≡ 5 mod 8. 5^2 = 25 ≡ 1 mod 8. So 5^2015 = 5^(2×1007+1) = (5^2)^1007 × 5 ≡ 1 × 5 = 5 mod 8.\nSo M ≡ 0 + 5 = 5 mod 8.\n\nM mod 15625: 15625 = 5^6.\n\nI need 12^2015 + 13^2015 mod 5^6.\n\nThis is the harder part. Let me think about how to compute this.\n\nLet me set up. We have a = 12, b = 13, a + b = 25 = 5^2.\n\nLet me write 12 = 25/2 - 1/2... hmm, that's not clean. Let me think differently.\n\nActually, let me use the substitution. Let me write 13 = 25 - 12, so 13 ≡ -12 mod 25 but that's mod 25 not helpful directly for mod 5^6.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. I'll compute 12^2015 + 13^2015 mod 5^6 = 15625.\n\nSince 12 + 13 = 25, let me set s = 12, and 13 = 25 - 12. Hmm, but 25 - 12 = 13. So 13 = 25 - 12.\n\nSo 12^2015 + 13^2015 = 12^2015 + (25 - 12)^2015.\n\nLet me expand (25 - 12)^2015 using binomial theorem:\n(25 - 12)^2015 = Σ_{k=0}^{2015} C(2015,k) 25^k (-12)^{2015-k}\n\nSo 12^2015 + (25-12)^2015 = 12^2015 + Σ_{k=0}^{2015} C(2015,k) 25^k (-12)^{2015-k}\n\nThe k=0 term is (-12)^2015 = -12^2015 (since 2015 is odd).\n\nSo 12^2015 + (-12^2015) = 0, and we're left with:\n\n12^2015 + 13^2015 = Σ_{k=1}^{2015} C(2015,k) 25^k (-12)^{2015-k}\n\n= Σ_{k=1}^{2015} C(2015,k) 25^k (-1)^{2015-k} 12^{2015-k}\n\nNow 25^k = 5^{2k}. We want this mod 5^6, so terms with 2k ≥ 6, i.e., k ≥ 3, vanish mod 5^6.\n\nSo mod 5^6:\n\n12^2015 + 13^2015 ≡ Σ_{k=1}^{2} C(2015,k) 25^k (-1)^{2015-k} 12^{2015-k} mod 5^6\n\nk=1: C(2015,1) × 25 × (-1)^{2014} × 12^{2014} = 2015 × 25 × 12^{2014}\nk=2: C(2015,2) × 625 × (-1)^{2013} × 12^{2013} = -C(2015,2) × 625 × 12^{2013}\n\nSo M ≡ 2015 × 25 × 12^{2014} - C(2015,2) × 625 × 12^{2013} mod 5^6.\n\nLet me compute each term mod 5^6 = 15625.\n\nFirst, let me note:\n- 2015 = 5 × 403\n- C(2015, 2) = 2015 × 2014 / 2 = 2015 × 1007\n\nTerm 1: 2015 × 25 × 12^{2014} = 5 × 403 × 25 × 12^{2014} = 125 × 403 × 12^{2014}\n\nSo Term 1 = 125 × 403 × 12^{2014}.\n\nTerm 2: C(2015,2) × 625 × 12^{2013} = 2015 × 1007 × 625 × 12^{2013} = 5 × 403 × 1007 × 625 × 12^{2013} = 3125 × 403 × 1007 × 12^{2013}\n\nSo Term 2 = 3125 × 403 × 1007 × 12^{2013}.\n\nNow M ≡ Term1 - Term2 mod 15625.\n\nM = 125 × 403 × 12^{2014} - 3125 × 403 × 1007 × 12^{2013} mod 15625\n\nFactor out 125 × 12^{2013}:\n\nM = 125 × 12^{2013} × [403 × 12 - 25 × 403 × 1007] mod 15625\n\nLet me compute the bracket: 403 × 12 - 25 × 403 × 1007 = 403 × (12 - 25 × 1007) = 403 × (12 - 25175) = 403 × (-25163)\n\nSo M = 125 × 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163) mod 15625.\n\nNow 15625 = 125 × 125. So M/125 = 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163) mod 125.\n\nSo I need 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163) mod 125.\n\nLet me compute each factor mod 125.\n\n403 mod 125 = 403 - 3×125 = 403 - 375 = 28.\n\n25163 mod 125: 25163 / 125 = 201.304, 201 × 125 = 25125, 25163 - 25125 = 38. So 25163 ≡ 38 mod 125. So -25163 ≡ -38 ≡ 87 mod 125.\n\n12^{2013} mod 125: I need to compute this. 125 = 5^3. φ(125) = 100. gcd(12, 125) = 1. So 12^{100} ≡ 1 mod 125.\n\n2013 mod 100 = 13. So 12^{2013} ≡ 12^{13} mod 125.\n\nLet me compute 12^{13} mod 125.\n\n12^1 = 12\n12^2 = 144 ≡ 144 - 125 = 19 mod 125\n12^4 = 19^2 = 361 ≡ 361 - 2×125 = 361 - 250 = 111 mod 125\n12^8 = 111^2 = 12321. 12321 / 125 = 98.568, 98 × 125 = 12250, 12321 - 12250 = 71. So 12^8 ≡ 71 mod 125.\n\n12^13 = 12^8 × 12^4 × 12^1 = 71 × 111 × 12 mod 125.\n\n71 × 111 = 7881. 7881 / 125 = 63.048, 63 × 125 = 7875, 7881 - 7875 = 6. So 71 × 111 ≡ 6 mod 125.\n\n6 × 12 = 72. So 12^{13} ≡ 72 mod 125.\n\nSo 12^{2013} ≡ 72 mod 125.\n\nNow M/125 ≡ 72 × 28 × 87 mod 125.\n\n72 × 28 = 2016. 2016 / 125 = 16.128, 16 × 125 = 2000, 2016 - 2000 = 16. So 72 × 28 ≡ 16 mod 125.\n\n16 × 87 = 1392. 1392 / 125 = 11.136, 11 × 125 = 1375, 1392 - 1375 = 17. So 16 × 87 ≡ 17 mod 125.\n\nSo M/125 ≡ 17 mod 125.\n\nWait, but I need to be careful. Let me recheck.\n\nM ≡ 125 × 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163) mod 15625.\n\nM/125 ≡ 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163) mod 125.\n\nBut wait - I need M/125 mod 1000, not mod 125. Let me reconsider.\n\nI need M/125 mod 1000. M/125 mod 1000 requires M mod 125000.\n\nI set up: 125000 = 8 × 15625, and I need M mod 8 and M mod 15625.\n\nM mod 8 = 5 (computed above).\n\nM mod 15625: I computed M ≡ 125 × [12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163)] mod 15625.\n\nLet me denote B = 12^{2013} × 403 × (-25163). Then M ≡ 125 × B mod 15625.\n\nSo M/125 ≡ B mod 125 (since 15625/125 = 125).\n\nSo M/125 ≡ 17 mod 125 (from above).\n\nNow I also need M/125 mod 8. \n\nM mod 8 = 5, and 125 mod 8 = 5. So M/125 mod 8... but I need to be careful. M ≡ 5 mod 8, and 125 ≡ 5 mod 8. Since 125 | M, M = 125 × Q where Q = M/125. Then 125 × Q ≡ 5 mod 8, so 5 × Q ≡ 5 mod 8, so Q ≡ 1 mod 8 (since gcd(5,8)=1, 5^{-1} mod 8 = 5, so Q ≡ 5×5 = 25 ≡ 1 mod 8).\n\nSo M/125 ≡ 1 mod 8.\n\nNow by CRT: M/125 ≡ 17 mod 125 and M/125 ≡ 1 mod 8.\n\nFind x such that x ≡ 17 mod 125 and x ≡ 1 mod 8, with 0 ≤ x < 1000.\n\nx = 17 + 125k for some k. \n17 + 125k ≡ 1 mod 8.\n17 mod 8 = 1. 125 mod 8 = 5.\n1 + 5k ≡ 1 mod 8.\n5k ≡ 0 mod 8.\nk ≡ 0 mod 8 (since gcd(5,8)=1).\n\nSo k = 0, 8, 16, ... but x < 1000, so k can be 0 or 8 (k=8 gives x = 17 + 1000 = 1017 > 1000, no). Wait, k=0 gives x=17, k=8 gives x = 17 + 1000 = 1017 which is ≥ 1000. \n\nHmm wait, but we need x mod 1000, so x could be 17 or 17 + 1000 = 1017, but mod 1000 that's 17. Actually, the answer is x mod 1000. Since x ≡ 17 mod 1000 (because k ≡ 0 mod 8 means k = 8m, x = 17 + 1000m, so x ≡ 17 mod 1000).\n\nWait, that's interesti