{13} mod 125 carefully.\n\n12^1 = 12\n12^2 = 144 = 125 + 19, so 19 mod 125\n12^3 = 12 × 19 = 228 = 125 + 103, so 103 mod 125\n12^4 = 12 × 103 = 1236 = 9×125 + 111 = 1125 + 111, so 111 mod 125. \n\nWait let me redo: 12^4 = (12^2)^2 = 19^2 = 361 = 2×125 + 111 = 250 + 111, so 111 mod 125. ✓\n\n12^8 = (12^4)^2 = 111^2 = 12321. 12321 / 125 = 98.568. 98 × 125 = 12250. 12321 - 12250 = 71. So 71 mod 125.\n\n12^{13} = 12^8 × 12^4 × 12^1 = 71 × 111 × 12 mod 125.\n\n71 × 111: 71 × 111 = 71 × 100 + 71 × 11 = 7100 + 781 = 7881. 7881 / 125 = 63.048. 63 × 125 = 7875. 7881 - 7875 = 6. So 6 mod 125.\n\n6 × 12 = 72. So 12^{13} ≡ 72 mod 125. ✓\n\nNow: 72 × 28 × 87 mod 125.\n\n72 × 28 = 2016. 2016 / 125 = 16.128. 16 × 125 = 2000. 2016 - 2000 = 16. So 16 mod 125.\n\n16 × 87 = 1392. 1392 / 125 = 11.136. 11 × 125 = 1375. 1392 - 1375 = 17. So 17 mod 125. ✓\n\nSo M/125 ≡ 17 mod 125.\n\nAnd M/125 ≡ 1 mod 8.\n\nCRT: x ≡ 17 mod 125, x ≡ 1 mod 8.\n\n17 mod 8 = 1. ✓ So x = 17 works, and the answer is 17 mod 1000.\n\nLet me verify the LTE application once more. LTE for p=5, p | a+b, p ∤ a, p ∤ b, n odd:\nv_p(a^n + b^n) = v_p(a+b) + v_p(n)\n\na=12, b=13, a+b=25, v_5(25)=2. n=2015, v_5(2015)=1.\nv_5(12^2015 + 13^2015) = 2 + 1 = 3. ✓\n\nSo n = 3, and the remainder is 17.\n\nLet me also sanity check with a small example. Let's check 12^1 + 13^1 = 25 = 5^2. v_5 = 2. LTE: v_5(25) + v_5(1) = 2 + 0 = 2. ✓\n\n12^5 + 13^5: LTE says v_5 = v_5(25) + v_5(5) = 2 + 1 = 3.\n12^5 = 248832, 13^5 = 371293. Sum = 620125. 620125 / 125 = 4961. 4961 / 5 = 992.2, not divisible. So v_5 = 3. ✓\n\nAnd 620125 / 125 = 4961. 4961 mod 1000 = 961.\n\nLet me check with my formula for k=5 (instead of 2015). Actually, my formula was specific to 2015, so let me not bother. The method checks out.\n\nActually, let me verify the answer differently. Let me verify that 12^2015 + 13^2015 ≡ 125 × 17 = 2125 mod 1000... no wait, that's not right. M/125 ≡ 17 mod 1000 means M ≡ 125 × 17 = 2125 mod 125000. 2125 mod 125000 = 2125. And 2125 mod 